is Poisson with mean <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\bar{\lambda}_{\xi,\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\bar{\lambda}_{\xi,\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the
reference gradient is already cached, the capped simulation makes
exactly one gradient query at each arrival. This proves
(<a href="#S4.E7" title="Equation 4.7 ‣ Item (ii) ‣ Proposition 4.2 (Capped-rate approximation and query cost). ‣ 4.2 Simulating the half-turn process ‣ 4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Operator formulation and modified <math id="A2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> contraction</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix proves <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (𝜒^2 convergence). ‣ 3.3 Ideal Proximal BPS ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> using the modified <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
hypocoercivity framework of Dolbeault, Mouhot, and Schmeiser <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> and Fan, Li, and Lu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. We first set
up the operator framework and then derive the contraction from two
model-specific estimates proved in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Operator framework and micro–macro decomposition</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our target distribution is the joint law <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in
(<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 2.6 ‣ 2.2 Proximal augmentation and the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>), and we set <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}\coloneqq L^{2}(\pi_{\eta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}\coloneqq L^{2}(\pi_{\eta})</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall from
(<a href="#S3.E11" title="Equation 3.11 ‣ 3.3 Ideal Proximal BPS ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>) that the ideal Proximal BPS has
transition kernel</p>
<table id="A2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}\bigl((x,y),\mathrm{d}x^{\prime}\,\mathrm{d}y^{\prime}\bigr)=\delta_{2x-y}(\mathrm{d}y^{\prime})\,\bigl[\rho\,\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{\prime}}(\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})+(1-\rho)\,\mathcal{H}_{y^{\prime}}(x,\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})\bigr]\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.150em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}\bigl((x,y),\mathrm{d}x^{\prime}\,\mathrm{d}y^{\prime}\bigr)=\delta_{2x-y}(\mathrm{d}y^{\prime})\,\bigl[\rho\,\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{\prime}}(\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})+(1-\rho)\,\mathcal{H}_{y^{\prime}}(x,\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})\bigr]\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{y^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{y^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the conditional half-turn kernel from
<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 3.2 (Reversibility of the conditional half-turn kernel). ‣ 3.2 The ideal conditional half-turn process ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. Since the algorithm is a discrete-time
chain, its evolution is described by the induced one-step Markov operator
on observables,</p>
<table id="A2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{K}f)(x,y)\coloneqq\int_{\mathbb{R}^{2d}}f(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})\,\mathcal{K}\bigl((x,y),\mathrm{d}x^{\prime}\,\mathrm{d}y^{\prime}\bigr)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{K}f)(x,y)\coloneqq\int_{\mathbb{R}^{2d}}f(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})\,\mathcal{K}\bigl((x,y),\mathrm{d}x^{\prime}\,\mathrm{d}y^{\prime}\bigr)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on probability laws, whereas <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is its
induced action on observables. The first term in brackets
in <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is the conditional resampling branch. After the
auxiliary coordinate is updated to <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,
this branch replaces the <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-coordinate by an independent draw from
<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The associated conditional expectation operator
is</p>
<table id="A2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{P}f)(x,y)\coloneqq\int f(x^{\prime},y)\,\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}(\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})=\mathbb{E}[f(X,Y)\mid Y=y]\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{P}f)(x,y)\coloneqq\int f(x^{\prime},y)\,\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}(\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})=\mathbb{E}[f(X,Y)\mid Y=y]\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">In particular, <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{P}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{P}f</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on the coordinate <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. The range
and kernel of <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{P}</annotation></semantics></math> are</p>
<table id="A2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}&amp;\coloneqq\operatorname{ran}(\mathsf{P})=\{f\in\mathfrak{H}:f(x,y)=g(y)\text{ for some }g\in L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})\}\,,\\
\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}&amp;\coloneqq\ker(\mathsf{P})=\operatorname{ran}(\mathsf{P}^{\perp})=\{f\in\mathfrak{H}:\mathbb{E}[f(X,Y)\mid Y]=0\}\,.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>ran</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℌ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for some </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.300em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ran</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℌ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.022em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}&amp;\coloneqq\operatorname{ran}(\mathsf{P})=\{f\in\mathfrak{H}:f(x,y)=g(y)\text{ for some }g\in L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})\}\,,\\
\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}&amp;\coloneqq\ker(\mathsf{P})=\operatorname{ran}(\mathsf{P}^{\perp})=\{f\in\mathfrak{H}:\mathbb{E}[f(X,Y)\mid Y]=0\}\,.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\coloneqq I-\mathsf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\coloneqq I-\mathsf{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}=\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\oplus\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}=\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\oplus\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\mathsf{P}f+\mathsf{P}^{\perp}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\mathsf{P}f+\mathsf{P}^{\perp}f</annotation></semantics></math>. In the common terminology of
hypocoercivity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{ran}(\mathsf{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ran</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{ran}(\mathsf{P})</annotation></semantics></math> is the
<em id="A2.SS1.p1.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">macroscopic</em> subspace and its orthogonal complement is the
<em id="A2.SS1.p1.5.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">microscopic</em> subspace.
Thus functions in <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> depend only on the coordinate <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>,
whereas functions in <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> have zero mean under
<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>-almost every <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Relative to the decomposition
<math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}=\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\oplus\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}=\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\oplus\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>, we write an operator
<math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℌ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}</annotation></semantics></math> in block form as</p>
<table id="A2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{T}=\begin{pmatrix}\mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{P}\perp}\\
\mathsf{T}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{T}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\begin{aligned} \mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}&amp;\coloneqq\mathsf{P}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{P}\,,&amp;\mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{P}\perp}&amp;\coloneqq\mathsf{P}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\,,\\
\mathsf{T}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}&amp;\coloneqq\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{P}\,,&amp;\mathsf{T}_{\perp\perp}&amp;\coloneqq\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\,.\end{aligned}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mi>𝖯𝖳𝖯</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><msup><mi>𝖯𝖳𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖳𝖯</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝖳𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{T}=\begin{pmatrix}\mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{P}\perp}\\
\mathsf{T}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{T}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\begin{aligned} \mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}&amp;\coloneqq\mathsf{P}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{P}\,,&amp;\mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{P}\perp}&amp;\coloneqq\mathsf{P}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\,,\\
\mathsf{T}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}&amp;\coloneqq\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{P}\,,&amp;\mathsf{T}_{\perp\perp}&amp;\coloneqq\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\,.\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The first subscript specifies the target subspace and the second the source
subspace; thus <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{T}_{ab}:\mathfrak{H}_{b}\to\mathfrak{H}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{T}_{ab}:\mathfrak{H}_{b}\to\mathfrak{H}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="A2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b\in\{\mathsf{P},\perp\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⟂</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b\in\{\mathsf{P},\perp\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We first apply this block notation to the reflection map <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}</annotation></semantics></math> from
(<a href="#S2.E14" title="Equation 2.14 ‣ Item (iii) ‣ Proposition 2.1 (Geometry of the joint law). ‣ 2.2 Proximal augmentation and the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.14</span></a>). We use the same symbol for its pullback on
observables:</p>
<table id="A2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{U}f)(x,y)\coloneqq f(\mathsf{U}(x,y))=f(x,2x-y)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖴</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{U}f)(x,y)\coloneqq f(\mathsf{U}(x,y))=f(x,2x-y)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This pullback is self-adjoint and unitary, and hence has block matrix</p>
<table id="A2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}=\begin{pmatrix}\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\
\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{U}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}=\begin{pmatrix}\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\
\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{U}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}=\mathsf{P}\mathsf{U}\mathsf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖯𝖴𝖯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}=\mathsf{P}\mathsf{U}\mathsf{P}</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> to itself, whereas
<math id="A2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{U}\mathsf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖴𝖯</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{U}\mathsf{P}</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since
<math id="A2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is self-adjoint and unitary, <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a self-adjoint
contraction on <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Writing <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}^{2}=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖴</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}^{2}=I</annotation></semantics></math> in blocks gives</p>
<table id="A2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\perp}=-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\perp}=-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We next express the joint half-turn operator <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖧</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{H}</annotation></semantics></math> in the same block
decomposition. It is defined in (<a href="#A1.E9" title="Equation A.9 ‣ Proposition A.2 (Joint measurability and augmented space half-turn operator). ‣ A.2 The averaged ideal half-turn kernel ‣ Appendix A Conditional half-turn process: construction, invariance, and implementation ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.9</span></a>) and is a
self-adjoint Markov contraction by
<a href="#A1.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition A.2 (Joint measurability and augmented space half-turn operator). ‣ A.2 The averaged ideal half-turn kernel ‣ Appendix A Conditional half-turn process: construction, invariance, and implementation ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>. Since <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖧</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{H}</annotation></semantics></math> acts only on the
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-coordinate, while every
function in <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{ran}(\mathsf{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ran</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{ran}(\mathsf{P})</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, we have
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{H}\mathsf{P}=\mathsf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖧𝖯</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{H}\mathsf{P}=\mathsf{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. Self-adjointness also gives
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{P}\mathsf{H}=\mathsf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖯𝖧</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{P}\mathsf{H}=\mathsf{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. Set
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\coloneqq\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{H}\mathsf{P}^{\perp}:\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}\to\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝖧𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\coloneqq\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{H}\mathsf{P}^{\perp}:\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}\to\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The identity <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{P}\mathsf{H}=\mathsf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖯𝖧</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{P}\mathsf{H}=\mathsf{P}</annotation></semantics></math> makes <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> invariant under
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖧</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> is its restriction to <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is
therefore self-adjoint, and
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}g\|=\|\mathsf{H}g\|\leq\|g\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝖧</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}g\|=\|\mathsf{H}g\|\leq\|g\|</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently,</p>
<table id="A2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathsf{H}=\mathsf{P}+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt-I\preceq\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\preceq I\hskip 10.00002pt\text{on }\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖧</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝖧</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="1.778em">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>⪯</mo><mi>𝖧</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⪯</mo><mi>I</mi><mtext>on </mtext><mi>ℌ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>⟂</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{H}=\mathsf{P}+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt-I\preceq\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\preceq I\hskip 10.00002pt\text{on }\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Relative to <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}=\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\oplus\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}=\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\oplus\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>, this decomposition
has the block representation</p>
<table id="A2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{H}=\begin{pmatrix}I_{\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}}&amp;0\\
0&amp;\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖧</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{H}=\begin{pmatrix}I_{\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}}&amp;0\\
0&amp;\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">The Markov operator <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by the transition kernel <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math>
therefore factors as</p>
<table id="A2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}=\mathsf{U}\bigl[\rho\mathsf{P}+(1-\rho)\mathsf{H}\bigr]=\mathsf{U}\bigl[\mathsf{P}+(1-\rho)\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\bigr]\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖪</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖧</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}=\mathsf{U}\bigl[\rho\mathsf{P}+(1-\rho)\mathsf{H}\bigr]=\mathsf{U}\bigl[\mathsf{P}+(1-\rho)\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\bigr]\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, its block representation is</p>
<table id="A2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}=\begin{pmatrix}\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\
\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{U}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}I_{\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}}&amp;0\\
0&amp;(1-\rho)\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖪</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}=\begin{pmatrix}\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\\
\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}&amp;\mathsf{U}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}I_{\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}}&amp;0\\
0&amp;(1-\rho)\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\end{pmatrix}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.5" class="ltx_p">The diagonal factor in the block representation of <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}</annotation></semantics></math> contracts
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> but leaves <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> unchanged. For an observable
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, contraction therefore relies on the
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>-component <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f</annotation></semantics></math> created by reflection. The next
subsection estimates this component and the subsequent action of
<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⟂</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin with the reflection operator <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that
<math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x,y)=f(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x,y)=f(y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Although <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> initially depends only on the auxiliary
coordinate <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, reflection gives <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{U}f)(x,y)=f(2x-y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖴</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{U}f)(x,y)=f(2x-y)</annotation></semantics></math>, which generally depends on both coordinates. Its macroscopic and microscopic
components are <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The following
probabilistic representation identifies the squared norm of the microscopic
component created by reflection with an expected conditional variance, thereby
enabling quantitative estimate by the conditional Poincaré estimates used in
Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Concretely, under <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>, write</p>
<table id="A2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{+}\coloneqq X+\sqrt{\eta}\,Z\,,\hskip 20.00003ptY_{-}\coloneqq X-\sqrt{\eta}\,Z\,,\hskip 20.00003ptZ\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)\,,\hskip 20.00003ptX\sim\mu\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo rspace="0.150em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{+}\coloneqq X+\sqrt{\eta}\,Z\,,\hskip 20.00003ptY_{-}\coloneqq X-\sqrt{\eta}\,Z\,,\hskip 20.00003ptZ\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)\,,\hskip 20.00003ptX\sim\mu\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The pair <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Y_{+},Y_{-})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Y_{+},Y_{-})</annotation></semantics></math> is exchangeable. We henceforth identify
<math id="A2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> and write <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(y)</annotation></semantics></math> for a
macroscopic observable. Under this identification,</p>
<table id="A2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f)(y)=\mathbb{E}[f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y]\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\mathbb{E}\Var(f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+})\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.150em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}f)(y)=\mathbb{E}[f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y]\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|^{2}=\mathbb{E}\Var(f(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+})\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify the microscopic component <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f</annotation></semantics></math> created when reflection
acts on <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="A2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\coloneqq(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2})^{1/2}\hskip 10.00002pt\text{on }\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathvariant="sans-serif">Γ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>on </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\coloneqq(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2})^{1/2}\hskip 10.00002pt\text{on }\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">By (<a href="#A2.E5" title="Equation B.5 ‣ B.1 Operator framework and micro–macro decomposition ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>),
<math id="A2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence</p>
<table id="A2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|=\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}f\|\,,\hskip 10.00002ptf\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathvariant="sans-serif">Γ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathvariant="sans-serif">Γ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.150em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}^{*}\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\|\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}f\|=\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}f\|\,,\hskip 10.00002ptf\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P}}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Thus a lower bound for <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif" mathvariant="sans-serif">Γ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies how much of a macroscopic
observable reflection transfers into the microscopic subspace.</p>
</div>
